224
00:12:14.252 --> 00:12:16.617
Hmm. It's an R.F. proximity card.

225
00:12:16.720 --> 00:12:20.484
- And that would be-
- Basically. it's a super-fancy key card.

226
00:12:20.589 --> 00:12:22.523
You wave the card
in front of the reader...

227
00:12:22.624 --> 00:12:25.114
it logs your info. and the door opens.

228
00:12:25.225 --> 00:12:27.420
Any way to figure out what door it opens?

229
00:12:27.527 --> 00:12:31.427
Well. if the info on the card isn't
too heavily encrypted.

230
00:12:31.529 --> 00:12:34.622
I heard you tried to get Hodgins
interested in doing an experiment.

231
00:12:34.731 --> 00:12:36.755
Let's just say I planted the seed.

232
00:12:38.833 --> 00:12:41.232
<i>Huh.</i>

233
00:12:41.335 --> 00:12:43.700
- What? C.I. A? N.S. A?
- No.

234
00:12:43.803 --> 00:12:45.929
Dupont Circle.

235
00:13:10.886 --> 00:13:13.251
<i>Special Agent Booth. F.B.I.</i>

236
00:13:16.022 --> 00:13:18.012
Ah!

237
00:13:18.123 --> 00:13:20.318
- Welcome to the Woodbury School. Agent Booth.
- Hi. How are you?

238
00:13:20.425 --> 00:13:22.689
- What's with all the security?
- It's precautionary.

239
00:13:22.794 --> 00:13:27.195
Our student body includes children
of diplomats. heads of state. C.E.O.'s.

240
00:13:27.296 --> 00:13:29.195
<i>- Good morning.
- How old is your child?</i>

241
00:13:29.297 --> 00:13:31.321
Parker? Oh. he's seven.

242
00:13:31.432 --> 00:13:33.661
- Has he been evaluated?
- He does just fine.

243
00:13:33.766 --> 00:13:36.893
Well. most applicant families
engage an educational consultant.

244
00:13:37.002 --> 00:13:39.902
- I mean. it's not a requirement. of course. but-
- Mr. Donnegan...

245
00:13:40.004 --> 00:13:43.961
- I think you got this all wrong.
- I know it seems crazy. but given the demand...

246
00:13:44.073 --> 00:13:47.235
we can only accept
the most qualified applicants.

247
00:13:47.342 --> 00:13:49.775
Oh. Parker's a great kid.

248
00:13:49.876 --> 00:13:52.309
The problem is keeping him
that way. isn't it?

249
00:13:52.411 --> 00:13:55.810
Sixty-eight percent of our students
matriculate to Ivy League universities...

250
00:13:55.913 --> 00:13:59.370
and it's not because of our
electron microscope or A.P. classes.

251
00:13:59.482 --> 00:14:02.177
It's because of our honor code.

252
00:14:02.284 --> 00:14:05.275
<i>Any violation results
in immediate dismissal.</i>

253
00:14:05.386 --> 00:14:07.853
Now. why don't I get you
an admissions packet?

254
00:14:07.954 --> 00:14:10.387
A- Actually. I-

255
00:14:10.488 --> 00:14:12.456
I'm here because
of a murder investigation.

256
00:14:12.557 --> 00:14:15.820
- I'm sorry?
- One of your parking lot access cards...

257
00:14:15.925 --> 00:14:19.121
was found in the apartment
of a victim- a Cal Warren?

258
00:14:20.527 --> 00:14:23.324
He's not a parent or an employee.
I would know the name.

259
00:14:23.429 --> 00:14:27.455
- Maybe we could check your computer.
- Of course.

260
00:14:27.566 --> 00:14:31.056
- No one is issued a key card
without security clearance.

261
00:14:31.167 --> 00:14:33.100
- Thanks.
- One of our fathers...

262
00:14:33.202 --> 00:14:35.692
actually implemented the security system.

263
00:14:35.803 --> 00:14:38.430
He's deputy director of the C.I.A.

264
00:14:38.538 --> 00:14:40.438
- Really?
- Mm-hmm.

265
00:14:43.575 --> 00:14:45.838
There he is- Calvin Warren.

266
00:14:45.943 --> 00:14:47.501
Did he happen to work for the C.I. A?

267
00:14:47.611 --> 00:14:50.010
No. actually. he works for the president.

268
00:14:50.113 --> 00:14:52.478
Of the United States?

269
00:14:52.581 --> 00:14:54.844
Parents Association.

270
00:14:54.949 --> 00:14:58.008
- As a nanny.
- A nanny?

271
00:15:05.422 --> 00:15:07.321
<i>Twenty-eight grand a year.</i>

272
00:15:07.423 --> 00:15:09.322
I didn't pay that much
for four years of college.

273
00:15:09.424 --> 00:15:12.017
Well. if you think it's so ridiculous.
why did you save the application?

274
00:15:12.126 --> 00:15:15.117
I didn't save it. I just didn't have
a chance to throw it out yet.

275
00:15:15.228 --> 00:15:17.127
You don't have to be embarrassed.

276
00:15:17.229 --> 00:15:19.628
It's perfectly normal
to want the best for Parker.

277
00:15:19.730 --> 00:15:22.357
Public school was good enough for me.
It's good enough for my kid.

278
00:15:22.465 --> 00:15:24.227
Of course it is... probably.

279
00:15:25.733 --> 00:15:28.065
Mmm. what's that supposed to mean?

280
00:15:28.169 --> 00:15:31.603
Parker is a bright. engaged little boy.

281
00:15:31.704 --> 00:15:34.467
I'm sure he'll do fine
in a large classroom.

282
00:15:34.572 --> 00:15:37.563
<i>- I did.
- Except-</i>

283
00:15:37.675 --> 00:15:39.733
- Except what?
- Your dad was a science teacher.

284
00:15:39.842 --> 00:15:42.139
<i>- You're a scientist.
- Yes.</i>

285
00:15:42.244 --> 00:15:45.576
<i>- My education was enriched at home.
- That's what I gotta do-</i>

286
00:15:45.680 --> 00:15:49.170
enrich Parker at home.

287
00:15:49.281 --> 00:15:53.340
In what academics are you qualified
to offer enrichment?

288
00:15:53.450 --> 00:15:55.645
Well-

289
00:16:06.425 --> 00:16:08.688
<i>Ethel. can you grab my bag. please?</i>

290
00:16:08.793 --> 00:16:11.226
I am on my way to
the Children First board meeting.

291
00:16:11.328 --> 00:16:14.387
<i>Thank you.
Oh. Wexler has the groomer at 3:00.</i>

292
00:16:14.496 --> 00:16:16.360
Excuse me. Elsbeth King?

293
00:16:16.464 --> 00:16:18.829
- Yes?
- We're with the F.B.I.

294
00:16:18.932 --> 00:16:21.399
<i>Do you know a Mr. Calvin Warren?</i>

295
00:16:21.500 --> 00:16:24.229
He was my nanny. What happened?

296
00:16:24.336 --> 00:16:27.202
That's exactly what we're investigating.

297
00:16:27.304 --> 00:16:29.703
Cal dropped off the face
of the earth on Sunday.

298
00:16:29.806 --> 00:16:32.035
He was an excellent nanny.

299
00:16:32.140 --> 00:16:36.939
Well. how is it that an ex-Special
Ops guy becomes a nanny?

300
00:16:37.043 --> 00:16:40.068
My husband. Richard. handles defense
contracts- Middle East. South America.

301
00:16:40.179 --> 00:16:43.045
There were kidnap threats a couple
years ago. and Richard was concerned.

302
00:16:43.147 --> 00:16:45.205
He offered Cal 2.500 a week.

303
00:16:45.315 --> 00:16:48.578
<i>That's very good pay for a babysitter.</i>

304
00:16:48.683 --> 00:16:52.584
<i>Cal was more like a bodyguard.
Do you suppose that's what got him killed?</i>

305
00:16:52.686 --> 00:16:55.848
- Someone after my children?
- Um-

306
00:16:55.954 --> 00:16:59.013
- Could we speak to your children?
- Of course.

307
00:17:01.524 --> 00:17:04.856
Okay. so what time
on Sunday did you last see Cal?

308
00:17:04.960 --> 00:17:08.724
- Why?
- Because it's pertinent to our investigation.

309
00:17:12.031 --> 00:17:14.590
<i>- What?
- It's Mandarin Chinese.</i>

310
00:17:14.699 --> 00:17:16.791
My father says the Chinese will run
the planet in 10 years.

311
00:17:16.900 --> 00:17:18.867
He figures it'll be useful.

312
00:17:18.968 --> 00:17:23.461
- That's very smart.
- Okay. well. here. today- Okay? I run the world.

313
00:17:23.571 --> 00:17:26.038
Huh?

314
00:17:26.139 --> 00:17:29.232
He just called you a stupid. inbred sack of meat.

315
00:17:29.341 --> 00:17:31.467
Really?

316
00:17:31.576 --> 00:17:33.907
I'm sorry. Do you have
something to say to me?

317
00:17:39.481 --> 00:17:41.880
- He-He apologized.
- Good.

318
00:17:41.982 --> 00:17:44.745
So you want to tell me
what happened on Sunday?

319
00:17:44.851 --> 00:17:47.444
Well. Cal dropped me off at
the algebra tutor around 8:00...

320
00:17:47.552 --> 00:17:49.542
and then took Lexie
to check out a new horse.

321
00:17:49.653 --> 00:17:51.779
In the afternoon.
you had a game against All Saints.

322
00:17:51.888 --> 00:17:54.447
Lacrosse. I got this.

323
00:17:54.556 --> 00:17:56.455
Let's see.

324
00:17:56.557 --> 00:17:58.956
- Oh. And Cal took you to the emergency room?
- No.

325
00:17:59.059 --> 00:18:02.550
He knew one of the moms was a doctor.
so he figured she'd do a better job.

326
00:18:02.661 --> 00:18:04.491
Looks like the work of a plastic surgeon.

327
00:18:04.596 --> 00:18:07.291
No. dermatologist- Dr. Ezralow.

328
00:18:07.398 --> 00:18:10.127
<i>Very competent.</i>

329
00:18:12.434 --> 00:18:14.833
<i>The Royce boy was stitched up
by the same doctor...</i>

330
00:18:14.935 --> 00:18:17.061
who said she didn't know Cal Warren.

331
00:18:17.170 --> 00:18:19.262
Mm-hmm. I'm having
her brought in for questioning.

332
00:18:19.371 --> 00:18:21.497
You think those kids are
better off than Parker?

333
00:18:21.606 --> 00:18:24.267
Yeah. of course they are.
They have every advantage-

334
00:18:24.374 --> 00:18:26.273
a wonderful school. a successful father...

335
00:18:26.375 --> 00:18:28.275
mother committed to charity work.

336
00:18:28.377 --> 00:18:32.744
I spend more time with my house plants
than they do with their kids.

337
00:18:32.846 --> 00:18:35.746
Well. children of privilege
have always been raised by staff.

338
00:18:35.848 --> 00:18:38.042
That's how the upper one percent
stays the upper one percent.

339
00:18:38.150 --> 00:18:42.414
Assuming quality education
and adequate supervision...

340
00:18:42.519 --> 00:18:45.180
parents are actually rather irrelevant
beyond a certain age.

341
00:18:45.287 --> 00:18:47.811
- You're kidding me.
- Look at this.

342
00:18:47.922 --> 00:18:50.754
They start Latin in third grade.
That's fantastic.

343
00:18:50.857 --> 00:18:53.347
No. You know what's more important
than academic enrichment?

344
00:18:53.459 --> 00:18:55.358
A loving environment. You ask anyone.

345
00:18:55.460 --> 00:18:58.825
Parker is a wonderful child.
Booth. You shouldn't feel inadequate.

346
00:18:58.928 --> 00:19:01.021
Yeah. well. I'm perfectly capable
of raising my own kid.

347
00:19:01.130 --> 00:19:03.427
You're being defensive.

348
00:19:03.532 --> 00:19:05.761
- I am not.
- It's because you only have one child.

349
00:19:05.866 --> 00:19:08.527
When you procreate in multiples.
there's less pressure.

350
00:19:08.635 --> 00:19:11.329
Thank you. I feel much better.

351
00:19:16.173 --> 00:19:19.038
This way. Have a seat.

352
00:19:19.140 --> 00:19:21.369
I apologize. Agent Booth.

353
00:19:21.475 --> 00:19:24.705
When you called and asked
if I knew a Calvin Warren...

354
00:19:24.811 --> 00:19:27.472
I just didn't put it together
with the Kings' "manny. "

355
00:19:27.579 --> 00:19:30.012
<i>- Oh. so you did know him?
- Yes. butjust as Cal.</i>

356
00:19:30.114 --> 00:19:33.207
My son is on the same lacrosse team
as the King boy.

357
00:19:33.316 --> 00:19:36.148
- Did you have any more dealings with him?
- I'm afraid not.

358
00:19:37.385 --> 00:19:40.547
Actually. no.
That's not true. I'm sorry again.

359
00:19:40.654 --> 00:19:42.553
Calvin came along with us one time...

360
00:19:42.655 --> 00:19:44.645
when I took the King children
up for a ride.

361
00:19:44.756 --> 00:19:47.223
- A ride?
- I'm a pilot.

362
00:19:47.324 --> 00:19:49.314
I own a Seneca Five.

363
00:19:49.425 --> 00:19:53.292
<i>Cal got sick. and. well. the boys
thought that was hilarious.</i>

364
00:19:53.395 --> 00:19:57.352
You wouldn't happen to know how Cal Warren
got a prescription for painkillers from you?

365
00:19:57.464 --> 00:19:59.328
Oh. Isn't that obvious?

366
00:19:59.431 --> 00:20:04.366
<i>That's why he brought
Royce King to me to be stitched up-</i>

367
00:20:04.468 --> 00:20:06.992
to steal my prescription pad.

368
00:20:08.270 --> 00:20:09.861
Right. I have no further questions.

369
00:20:09.971 --> 00:20:11.870
So. you know. thanks for coming in.

370
00:20:11.972 --> 00:20:14.633
I just feel stupid for not being able
to put the face with the name.

371
00:20:14.740 --> 00:20:18.140
- I'm so sorry for wasting your time.
- No worries. Have a nice day.

372
00:20:20.544 --> 00:20:22.807
Hey. So you know
that thing where you insist...

373
00:20:22.912 --> 00:20:25.175
that all information goes through you?

374
00:20:25.280 --> 00:20:27.474
Yes. and I'm afraid
I'm immovable on the subject.

375
00:20:27.582 --> 00:20:30.379
And you send out little e-mails
summarizing the case so far?

376
00:20:30.484 --> 00:20:33.077
Yes. Plus. I check those e-mails to see
if they've been opened.

377
00:20:33.185 --> 00:20:35.084
That's a very good system.

378
00:20:35.186 --> 00:20:37.153
- Thank you.
- Because if you weren't checking...

379
00:20:37.254 --> 00:20:39.153
I probably never
would've read the e-mail...

380
00:20:39.255 --> 00:20:41.155
that said Booth questioned
a flying dermatologist.

381
00:20:41.257 --> 00:20:43.588
I hope you're not trying
to get some free lotion.

382
00:20:43.692 --> 00:20:46.057
I ran the accelerant samples
through the G.C. mass spec.

383
00:20:46.160 --> 00:20:49.787
- There were traces of tetraethyl lead.
- Have we changed the subject?

384
00:20:49.895 --> 00:20:52.294
No. Because if you hadn't been so anal
about the e-mails...

385
00:20:52.396 --> 00:20:54.295
I never would've known
that I discovered something...

386
00:20:54.397 --> 00:20:58.856
- that wasn't merely interesting. but vital to the investigation.
- Which is tetraethyl lead?

387
00:20:58.968 --> 00:21:01.833
Tetraethyl lead was a common
anti-knock additive in gasoline...

388
00:21:01.935 --> 00:21:04.732
that went out with catalytic
converters and unleaded gas.

389
00:21:04.837 --> 00:21:07.600
- Does the flying dermatologist drive a vintage car?
- Maybe.

390
00:21:07.706 --> 00:21:10.037
But for sure. the flying
dermatologist flies a plane.

391
00:21:10.140 --> 00:21:11.766
Aviation gas?

392
00:21:11.875 --> 00:21:15.037
The victim's remains were
incinerated by aviation gas.

393
00:21:15.143 --> 00:21:17.042
I'll let Booth know.

394
00:21:17.144 --> 00:21:19.611
Hopefully she hasn't flown the coop
while you were getting to the point.

395
00:21:20.714 --> 00:21:22.874
I was just trying to be nice.

396
00:21:28.886 --> 00:21:30.785
<i>- You lied to me.
- Yes.</i>

397
00:21:30.887 --> 00:21:33.116
I wrote Cal a prescription
for painkillers.

398
00:21:33.221 --> 00:21:35.188
<i>I shouldn't have.</i>

399
00:21:35.289 --> 00:21:37.518
- That's the truth.
- Well. not this time. Doc.

400
00:21:37.624 --> 00:21:39.818
Now that I know that
you're a world-class liar...

401
00:21:39.925 --> 00:21:43.587
I came just a little bit more
prepared this time.

402
00:21:43.694 --> 00:21:45.354
Take a look at those there.

403
00:21:45.462 --> 00:21:47.554
<i>- My flight logs.
- Mm-hmm.</i>

404
00:21:47.663 --> 00:21:49.721
<i>- For the past year.
- Yeah. Hmm.</i>

405
00:21:49.831 --> 00:21:52.390
Oh. lookee there. This one here.

406
00:21:52.499 --> 00:21:54.295
Right here.

407
00:21:54.400 --> 00:21:57.061
Yes. I took Cal flying a few times.

408
00:21:57.168 --> 00:21:59.260
Mm-hmm. Without the kids?

409
00:21:59.370 --> 00:22:01.634
<i>This one here is my favorite-</i>

410
00:22:01.739 --> 00:22:03.763
this particular flight to New York.

411
00:22:06.208 --> 00:22:08.801
You were gone the whole weekend.

412
00:22:12.378 --> 00:22:14.345
All right.

413
00:22:14.445 --> 00:22:16.504
All right. I'll tell you
what you want to know.

414
00:22:16.614 --> 00:22:20.048
No. My husband didn't know I was
having an affair with the Kings' manny.

415
00:22:20.149 --> 00:22:23.140
Would I have killed Cal
to prevent him from finding out?

416
00:22:23.251 --> 00:22:26.878
No. I would not.

417
00:22:26.986 --> 00:22:31.422
Why? Because my husband
doesn't give a damn who I boff.

418
00:22:32.757 --> 00:22:34.951
Do I know anyone else
who might wish Cal ill?

419
00:22:35.058 --> 00:22:38.049
Yes. Richard King.

420
00:22:38.160 --> 00:22:41.424
Don't stop. This-
This is great. Keep going.

421
00:22:41.529 --> 00:22:44.122
Cal told me to sell a stock.

422
00:22:44.231 --> 00:22:47.926
He said he had some insider knowledge
that this company was in trouble.

423
00:22:48.033 --> 00:22:50.000
I took the tip. made a killing.

424
00:22:50.100 --> 00:22:52.294
That's when I flew him
to New York to say thank you.

425
00:22:52.402 --> 00:22:56.565
And he said. "You're welcome"-
all weekend.

426
00:22:56.672 --> 00:22:59.935
What does this have to do with Richard King?

427
00:23:01.641 --> 00:23:03.938
It was Richard King's company.

428
00:23:12.213 --> 00:23:15.409
- How's that look. Max?
- Good. It looks good.

429
00:23:15.516 --> 00:23:17.449
I never thought I'd be doing
another experiment.

430
00:23:17.550 --> 00:23:19.450
Dad. what are you doing in here?

431
00:23:19.551 --> 00:23:21.451
Max had a great idea for an experiment.

432
00:23:21.553 --> 00:23:23.918
Dad. you said you wouldn't touch evidence.

433
00:23:24.021 --> 00:23:27.147
Uh. he's been very careful
not to touch the evidence. Dr. Brennan.

434
00:23:27.256 --> 00:23:29.815
<i>You know. your old man-
he reminded me why I got into science.</i>

435
00:23:29.924 --> 00:23:33.984
- To catch murderers?
- No. To figure stuff out in amusing ways.

436
00:23:34.094 --> 00:23:37.152
<i>The bones were incinerated
by aviation fuel.</i>

437
00:23:37.262 --> 00:23:39.957
They just need to know
how strong the wind was. Basic.

438
00:23:40.064 --> 00:23:42.156
So figure out how big a wind
blew the bones.

439
00:23:42.265 --> 00:23:46.428
<i>- Check the weather charts.
- Basic. That's very basic.</i>

440
00:23:46.535 --> 00:23:49.332
But this is the same wind tunnel
you helped me build for the science fair.

441
00:23:49.437 --> 00:23:51.165
Yeah. A basic wind tunnel. honey.

442
00:23:51.271 --> 00:23:53.261
<i>You won first prize.</i>

443
00:23:53.372 --> 00:23:56.306
I made casts that weigh the same
as the bones we found in that tree.

444
00:23:56.407 --> 00:24:00.705
- Now we put
the skull cast into the wind tunnel...

445
00:24:00.810 --> 00:24:03.437
since that was the
largest artifact on the tree.

446
00:24:04.645 --> 00:24:07.045
<i>I didn't touch anything.</i>

447
00:24:07.146 --> 00:24:11.548
The bone begins to move
at wind speeds of 42 miles per hour.

448
00:24:11.650 --> 00:24:14.674
Not enough net force to achieve aerodynamic lift.

449
00:24:14.784 --> 00:24:18.310
But crank it to 69-

450
00:24:21.756 --> 00:24:25.315
I checked the National Weather Service.

451
00:24:25.424 --> 00:24:27.186
<i>There were windstorms
in that area last week.</i>

452
00:24:27.292 --> 00:24:31.227
A strong nor'easter
hit 71 miles per hour late last Tuesday.

453
00:24:31.328 --> 00:24:34.592
<i>That gives us a 48-hour window
for Calvin Warren's murder.</i>

454
00:24:34.696 --> 00:24:37.687
But we still don't know where he was killed
or where the body was dumped.

455
00:24:37.798 --> 00:24:40.231
- Wait a minute. I can figure that out.
- Dad. you're fired.

456
00:24:40.333 --> 00:24:45.234
- What?
- The National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration.

457
00:24:45.336 --> 00:24:47.065
<i>- NOAA tracks surface conditions.
- Yeah.</i>

458
00:24:47.171 --> 00:24:49.331
<i>So we can calculate where
the bones started out...</i>

459
00:24:49.439 --> 00:24:51.338
if we can plot approximate mass...

460
00:24:51.440 --> 00:24:53.407
and reverse the path
of that nor'easter wind.

461
00:24:53.508 --> 00:24:57.306
<i>The heavier remains will still be near
the original dump site.</i>

462
00:24:57.410 --> 00:25:01.175
- You see? I'm helping.
- Oh. most definitely is helping.

463
00:25:01.279 --> 00:25:03.212
I'm sorry. Dad. but you're fired.

464
00:25:03.314 --> 00:25:05.304
You took part in a forensic experiment.

465
00:25:05.415 --> 00:25:07.314
You said you wouldn't. but you did!

466
00:25:07.416 --> 00:25:10.543
- So now you're fired.
- Dr. Brennan. he didn't touch any of the evidence.

467
00:25:10.652 --> 00:25:12.881
It's okay. kid.

468
00:25:12.986 --> 00:25:14.953
Never mind.

469
00:25:19.290 --> 00:25:22.883
<i>Nice assist. Nick.
Now cage him out. quick.</i>

470
00:25:22.992 --> 00:25:26.358
<i>He's open. Pass it! Pass it!</i>

471
00:25:26.461 --> 00:25:28.587
Hi. Alexa. How are you?

472
00:25:28.696 --> 00:25:31.858
Shouldn't you be. like. studying algebra
or Chinese or solving the world's problems?

473
00:25:31.965 --> 00:25:35.091
I'm cheering for Royce. He's the goalie.

474
00:25:35.199 --> 00:25:37.723
<i>Go! Go!</i>

475
00:25:37.834 --> 00:25:40.028
I watch football.
It's a better sport. It's American.

476
00:25:40.136 --> 00:25:42.331
Lacrosse was invented
by the American Indian.

477
00:25:42.437 --> 00:25:44.405
You can't get much more American than that.

478
00:25:46.306 --> 00:25:49.274
- Hi.
- Oh. God. What now?

479
00:25:49.375 --> 00:25:51.239
Well. I'm just thinking about this whole
insider trading thing.

480
00:25:51.343 --> 00:25:54.539
- I told you everything.
- I'm thinking. Cal. you know. gives you a tip on a stock.

481
00:25:54.645 --> 00:25:57.908
You sell it. You make two. three million dollars.
and it raises questions in my mind.

482
00:25:58.013 --> 00:26:02.539
<i>How did Richard King find out
that Cal spilled insider knowledge?</i>

483
00:26:02.649 --> 00:26:05.412
- That's right. Bones.
- I don't know.

484
00:26:05.518 --> 00:26:08.884
Well. where were you
between last Sunday and Tuesday night?

485
00:26:08.987 --> 00:26:12.353
- Who is she?
- Don't get jealous. Doc. Just answer the question.

486
00:26:12.455 --> 00:26:17.516
I was in San Antonio doing skin grafts
on a firefighter with third-degree burns.

487
00:26:17.625 --> 00:26:20.286
- You've got your own plane.
- You had time to fly back.

488
00:26:20.393 --> 00:26:23.555
You really want me to believe
that Richard King killed Cal?

489
00:26:23.662 --> 00:26:26.459
Cal Warren's remains
were incinerated with aviation gas.

490
00:26:26.564 --> 00:26:29.157
- I didn't kill Cal.
- You know what? She lies a lot.

491
00:26:29.266 --> 00:26:32.063
- I don't think she can help it at all.
- This is my lawyer's card.

492
00:26:32.168 --> 00:26:34.067
- Hmm.
- She's decided not to cooperate.

493
00:26:34.169 --> 00:26:37.898
No. I've decided to watch
my son play lacrosse.

494
00:26:41.640 --> 00:26:43.937
Okay. Here's the surface weather
for Tuesday night...

495
00:26:44.041 --> 00:26:46.804
when that nor'easter blew
the bones into that tree.

496
00:26:46.910 --> 00:26:50.400
Now that red "X"- that's the tree.

497
00:26:50.511 --> 00:26:52.944
- Mm-hmm.
- The bones must've traveled...

498
00:26:53.046 --> 00:26:56.412
along this vector.

499
00:26:56.515 --> 00:26:59.449
<i>Now given the duration of the gusts
and the terrain obstacles...</i>

500
00:26:59.551 --> 00:27:02.677
liftoff must've happened
somewhere between here...

501
00:27:02.785 --> 00:27:04.753
and here.

502
00:27:04.854 --> 00:27:08.811
That's on the edge of
Queen Anne's and Kent County.

503
00:27:08.923 --> 00:27:11.789
- Good job. Excellent experiment.
- Don't thank me.

504
00:27:11.891 --> 00:27:13.585
Thank Max Brennan.

505
00:27:20.563 --> 00:27:22.996
How's it going there. Max?

506
00:27:23.098 --> 00:27:25.066
It's the last day
with my kids this afternoon.

507
00:27:25.165 --> 00:27:27.724
- I'm toast.
- Cam fired you?

508
00:27:27.834 --> 00:27:30.064
No. no. Tempe fired me.

509
00:27:30.168 --> 00:27:32.261
And don't tell me
she hasn't got the authority...

510
00:27:32.370 --> 00:27:34.769
because believe me. I feel fired.

511
00:27:34.872 --> 00:27:37.431
Oh. Sorry. Max.

512
00:27:37.540 --> 00:27:40.905
- Can I ask you a question?
- Sure.

513
00:27:41.008 --> 00:27:44.602
You-Are you. uh-
Are you sleeping with my daughter?

514
00:27:44.711 --> 00:27:47.406
- No.
- Why? Are you gay?

515
00:27:47.513 --> 00:27:49.503
No.

516
00:27:49.614 --> 00:27:52.411
- Is she not attractive enough?
- Bones is beautiful.

517
00:27:52.516 --> 00:27:56.177
Is it because of me? Because I killed one man?
And we both know he deserved it.

518
00:27:56.284 --> 00:27:58.683
All right. just cut it out. Max.
All right. I'll talk to her.

519
00:27:58.786 --> 00:28:01.982
Probably not gonna get anywhere with her.
but I'll talk to her.

520
00:28:02.088 --> 00:28:04.612
You're a good man.

521
00:28:04.723 --> 00:28:06.622
And I want that for her.

522
00:28:06.724 --> 00:28:10.852
And now I gotta go
blow up some soda for some kids.

523
00:28:16.430 --> 00:28:18.829
Okay. according to the calculations...

524
00:28:18.931 --> 00:28:23.161
the bones must've started out
from somewhere in this vicinity.

525
00:28:23.267 --> 00:28:26.599
<i>- You know something. Bones?
It's tough being a father.</i>

526
00:28:26.703 --> 00:28:28.602
Parker will be fine.

527
00:28:28.704 --> 00:28:30.671
Peer groups and random
environmental factors...

528
00:28:30.772 --> 00:28:32.864
are far more important than family.

529
00:28:32.973 --> 00:28:35.634
<i>- How is that a good thing?
- Over here.</i>

530
00:28:35.741 --> 00:28:38.868
Agent Booth. over here.

531
00:28:42.045 --> 00:28:46.446
<i>- This must've been where the body was burned.
- You think?</i>

532
00:28:46.548 --> 00:28:50.448
Femur. pelvis. sternum.

533
00:28:50.550 --> 00:28:53.177
Gunshot trauma to the sternal angle.

534
00:28:53.285 --> 00:28:56.651
The buckshot embedded
in the anterior surface indicates...

535
00:28:56.754 --> 00:28:59.221
that the murder weapon
was a 12-gauge shotgun.

536
00:29:01.757 --> 00:29:04.020
<i>- But I thought Cal was strangled.
- He was-</i>

537
00:29:04.125 --> 00:29:06.558
<i>- Strangled. shot and set on fire.
- Whoa.</i>

538
00:29:06.660 --> 00:29:09.719
Someone wanted to make sure
he didn't get home.

539
00:29:09.829 --> 00:29:13.558
Hey. Bones. Look at that family crest.

540
00:29:13.664 --> 00:29:17.962
Looks like that someone came
from the Kings' country home.

541
00:29:28.773 --> 00:29:32.139
<i>Mr. King. we'd like to ask you
a few questions about your nanny.</i>

542
00:29:32.242 --> 00:29:35.233
I really don't see
how I can help you. Agent Booth.

543
00:29:35.344 --> 00:29:39.176
<i>- My wife deals with the staff.
- Do you own a 12-gauge shotgun?</i>

544
00:29:39.280 --> 00:29:42.874
Yeah. Several.
You're welcome to examine them.

545
00:29:42.982 --> 00:29:47.576
Two months ago. Cal gave an investor
insider knowledge about your company.

546
00:29:47.685 --> 00:29:50.244
- I didn't know that.
- And if you had known?

547
00:29:50.353 --> 00:29:53.878
- I suppose I would have tried to fire him.
- Tried?

548
00:29:53.988 --> 00:29:56.251
<i>- Well. I don't think that Elsbeth would have allowed it.
- Why?</i>

549
00:29:56.356 --> 00:29:59.882
- Was she having an affair with him?
- Oh. Bones- Good one.

550
00:29:59.992 --> 00:30:03.357
- You two need to learn focus.
- Excuse me?

551
00:30:03.460 --> 00:30:04.654
- Focus.
- Focus.

552
00:30:04.762 --> 00:30:06.456
Either accuse me of killing Cal...

553
00:30:06.563 --> 00:30:09.429
because he betrayed business secrets
or because he had sex with my wife.

554
00:30:09.531 --> 00:30:12.363
<i>And then once you've chosen.
then contact my lawyers.</i>

555
00:30:12.466 --> 00:30:14.456
I'd go with the business motive.

556
00:30:14.568 --> 00:30:17.092
I'm very happily married.

557
00:30:22.239 --> 00:30:24.297
Okay. that guy's one cool customer.

558
00:30:24.407 --> 00:30:28.432
- Ooh. Cool. like a- like a murderer?
- No. I don't know. okay?

559
00:30:28.542 --> 00:30:30.510
I just don't see him losing his temper...

560
00:30:30.611 --> 00:30:33.135
or taking on some Special Forces vet.
That's all.

561
00:30:33.245 --> 00:30:37.237
All right. do you think that dog
comes with a saddle. huh?

562
00:30:40.683 --> 00:30:42.776
Follow me. Bones. will ya. for a second?

563
00:30:47.120 --> 00:30:49.747
What's going on? What are you doing?

564
00:30:49.856 --> 00:30:51.618
Ahh.

565
00:30:53.191 --> 00:30:55.783
Are you gonna urinate in the gas
tank as an act of vandalism?

566
00:30:55.892 --> 00:30:57.792
Are you kidding me. Bones?

567
00:30:57.894 --> 00:31:01.191
I would never do something like that
to this fine Italian automobile.

568
00:31:02.296 --> 00:31:04.354
Beautiful car. It's Italian. Bones.

569
00:31:04.464 --> 00:31:07.557
Just remember that.
Italians make the best automobiles.

570
00:31:09.701 --> 00:31:11.861
Yeah. there it is.

571
00:31:13.970 --> 00:31:16.597
Okay. Bones. Give me. uh.
one of your little Baggies.

572
00:31:16.705 --> 00:31:18.604
- I didn't bring evidence bags.
- Thanks. Bones. Appreciate it.

573
00:31:18.706 --> 00:31:20.571
- You come prepared.
- We're not at a crime scene.

574
00:31:20.674 --> 00:31:22.573
- Why would I bring evidence bags?
- You always have evidence bags.

575
00:31:25.177 --> 00:31:28.633
<i>I've. um- I've decided I'm gonna
leave theJeffersonian.</i>

576
00:31:28.745 --> 00:31:31.042
- Why?
- Uh. because I fired him.

577
00:31:31.146 --> 00:31:34.637
- You can do that?
- Oh. yeah. Believe me. she can do it.

578
00:31:34.749 --> 00:31:37.979
<i>Well. it's all about proximity
to forensic evidence.</i>

579
00:31:38.085 --> 00:31:40.814
Uh. don't take any offense.
but that's a lot of crap.

580
00:31:40.919 --> 00:31:43.580
It's about a proximity to me. Right?

581
00:31:46.022 --> 00:31:47.922
Well. in my opinion...

582
00:31:48.024 --> 00:31:50.753
it isn't your father's presence
that's causing you anxiety.

583
00:31:50.859 --> 00:31:53.417
It's the memory of his absence.

584
00:31:53.526 --> 00:31:57.393
I can understand quantum mechanics.
but I can't understand you.

585
00:31:57.496 --> 00:32:00.986
<i>Well. make it easy.
I'm leaving theJeffersonian.</i>

586
00:32:01.098 --> 00:32:03.089
You are?

587
00:32:05.301 --> 00:32:08.269
- But I'm staying with you.
- What? Oh- In my house?

588
00:32:08.370 --> 00:32:10.269
No. No. no.

589
00:32:10.371 --> 00:32:14.669
- Wait. is that an offer?
- No. Would you-Would you want me to offer?

590
00:32:14.773 --> 00:32:17.104
No. no. But thanks for the offer.

591
00:32:17.208 --> 00:32:19.004
No. It wasn't an offer.
It was just a question.

592
00:32:19.109 --> 00:32:21.668
He isn't saying
he's literally staying with you.

593
00:32:21.777 --> 00:32:24.006
He's promising never to abandon you again.

594
00:32:28.448 --> 00:32:31.313
I'm sorry. but if it wasn't for
that evidence thing. I'd-

595
00:32:31.416 --> 00:32:34.077
- I'd let you keep your job.
- That's simply not true.

596
00:32:34.184 --> 00:32:36.174
<i>Hey. it's all right.</i>

597
00:32:37.120 --> 00:32:39.917
We're good.

598
00:32:40.022 --> 00:32:42.683
Uhh! Nothing I say has any impact.

599
00:32:42.790 --> 00:32:45.121
Uh. I should get back to work.

600
00:32:45.225 --> 00:32:47.454
Geez.

601
00:32:50.795 --> 00:32:53.024
Did she leave any money?

602
00:32:59.333 --> 00:33:02.165
- About the fractured hyoid.
- Yes?

603
00:33:02.268 --> 00:33:05.464
I was just remembering about a tapeworm.

604
00:33:05.570 --> 00:33:07.366
I'm gonna need more than that.

605
00:33:07.471 --> 00:33:11.337
There was a tapeworm in the line.
the one that went to the beer keg.

606
00:33:11.440 --> 00:33:15.068
I was working as a barback a couple years ago.
There's no flow from the tap line.

607
00:33:15.176 --> 00:33:18.610
- First assumption- Kink in the line. right?
- What else could it be?

608
00:33:18.711 --> 00:33:21.975
There was a tapeworm in
the line. eight inches long.

609
00:33:22.080 --> 00:33:24.479
- I learned something from that.
- Drink bottled beer?

610
00:33:24.581 --> 00:33:28.709
The tap don't flow.
you assume the line's got a kink.

611
00:33:28.817 --> 00:33:30.876
A guy's hyoid is broken...

612
00:33:30.986 --> 00:33:32.885
you assume he died from getting strangled.

613
00:33:32.987 --> 00:33:36.614
You found a tapeworm?
I mean metaphorically.

614
00:33:36.722 --> 00:33:39.349
Yeah. but I don't know if it's a tapeworm.
I need Dr. Brennan.

615
00:33:39.457 --> 00:33:41.822
Dr. Saroyan?

616
00:33:41.925 --> 00:33:44.688
I am so your most valuable player.

617
00:33:44.794 --> 00:33:47.660
<i>Now this is the profile
of the accelerant we found on the bones.</i>

618
00:33:47.762 --> 00:33:51.958
- Aviation gas.
- And this is the profile of the gas we took off Booth's tie.

619
00:33:53.433 --> 00:33:55.525
Richard King's car runs on "av" gas?

620
00:33:55.634 --> 00:33:58.397
He could've siphoned it out of his own car
to burn Cal Warren's remains.

621
00:33:58.502 --> 00:34:02.994
- And what is this anomaly?
- C6H807.

622
00:34:03.105 --> 00:34:05.834
<i>- Citric acid?
- It's my considered belief...</i>

623
00:34:05.940 --> 00:34:08.407
<i>that Agent Booth spilled orange juice on his tie.</i>

624
00:34:08.508 --> 00:34:10.737
I look forward to seeing him
explain that to a jury.

625
00:34:10.843 --> 00:34:12.742
Booth. it's Cam.

626
00:34:12.844 --> 00:34:17.575
I think you're gonna want to get yourself
a warrant to arrest Richard King.

627
00:34:17.680 --> 00:34:20.546
Fractures on the transverse
processes of the C2-

628
00:34:20.649 --> 00:34:22.912
both sides and the spinous process.

629
00:34:23.017 --> 00:34:24.813
And check out the occipital protuberance.

630
00:34:24.918 --> 00:34:29.080
- Oh. yes.
- Maybe this will help Booth keep Richard King in detention.

631
00:34:29.187 --> 00:34:32.815
Cal wasn't strangled postmortem.
He was dragged.

632
00:34:32.923 --> 00:34:36.379
Strangulation is the kink.
Dragging is the tapeworm.

633
00:34:36.491 --> 00:34:38.652
<i>The pattern of marks is evenly spaced...</i>

634
00:34:38.760 --> 00:34:42.888
- as if it were from a series of connected prongs.
- What would do that?

635
00:34:42.996 --> 00:34:46.794
- A choke chain.
- Richard King's a big strong guy.

636
00:34:46.897 --> 00:34:49.490
I don't see him dragging
a dead body around on a choke chain.

637
00:34:49.599 --> 00:34:51.896
That's because he didn't.

638
00:34:52.001 --> 00:34:54.833
The angle of the fracture is 18 degrees.

639
00:34:54.936 --> 00:34:56.835
Assuming a standard four-foot leash...

640
00:34:56.937 --> 00:34:59.564
whoever dragged
the victim is at most 5'5".

641
00:34:59.672 --> 00:35:01.264
Elsbeth King.

642
00:35:10.245 --> 00:35:12.178
So. you sure you don't want your attorney
present. Mrs. King?

643
00:35:12.279 --> 00:35:16.009
- Let's just get this over with.
- Okay. Sounds good to me.

644
00:35:16.116 --> 00:35:18.276
Here's. uh. what I'm thinking.

645
00:35:18.384 --> 00:35:21.909
I'm thinking that your country home
is less than a mile...

646
00:35:22.019 --> 00:35:24.452
from the spot where Cal's body was burned.

647
00:35:24.553 --> 00:35:28.318
You had access to the shotgun.
the aviation gas.

648
00:35:28.423 --> 00:35:30.947
You know how many people
work for us. Agent Booth?

649
00:35:31.058 --> 00:35:32.957
Every single one of them had access.

650
00:35:33.059 --> 00:35:35.856
Yeah. but I don't think any of them really
would have cared if they found out...

651
00:35:35.961 --> 00:35:40.089
that Cal Warren was trying to destroy
your husband's company.

652
00:35:43.399 --> 00:35:45.992
Hmm.

653
00:35:46.100 --> 00:35:48.624
You have a nice life.

654
00:35:48.735 --> 00:35:52.760
I'm sure you got really angry when
you found out that it was all going away.

655
00:35:52.871 --> 00:35:54.895
Hmm.

656
00:35:55.005 --> 00:35:57.529
Hmm?

657
00:35:57.640 --> 00:36:01.097
You don't understand.
It wasn't about the money.

658
00:36:02.510 --> 00:36:04.739
I had to protect my family.

659
00:36:08.347 --> 00:36:13.180
<i>- What do you think?
- In my professional opinion. she didn't whack him.</i>

660
00:36:13.283 --> 00:36:15.443
What? Come on. Cal betrayed them.

661
00:36:15.551 --> 00:36:17.950
Come on. Sweets. For once. you were right.

662
00:36:18.053 --> 00:36:20.111
- Embrace the wind. my friend.
- I'm sorry.

663
00:36:20.220 --> 00:36:23.552
But it's Kinesics 101. You know.
you pushed all the right buttons.

664
00:36:23.656 --> 00:36:26.987
But instead of her ego defense system
triggering a stress response state...

665
00:36:27.091 --> 00:36:29.558
<i>- she went straight to a tacit confession.
- Right.</i>

666
00:36:29.659 --> 00:36:31.922
Which means she did it.

667
00:36:32.027 --> 00:36:33.927
No. It means she didn't do it...

668
00:36:34.029 --> 00:36:36.292
but she wants you to believe that she did.

669
00:36:36.397 --> 00:36:40.126
- Why would she want to do that?
- 'Cause she's covering for someone.

670
00:36:40.232 --> 00:36:43.792
<i>The only honest thing that she said
was that she had to protect her family.</i>

671
00:36:43.902 --> 00:36:46.835
- So we're back to square one.
- Sort of.

672
00:36:46.936 --> 00:36:49.233
Geez.

673
00:36:51.173 --> 00:36:54.732
<i>Sweets. let me ask you something. Uh-</i>

674
00:36:54.841 --> 00:36:57.240
I mean. you're a smart guy.
You went to private school. right?

675
00:36:57.342 --> 00:37:00.640
Yeah. With perfect attendance
through sixth grade. might I add.

676
00:37:00.745 --> 00:37:03.008
Yeah. that's great. Um-

677
00:37:03.113 --> 00:37:05.012
You don't think that I'm a lousy dad...

678
00:37:05.114 --> 00:37:07.513
for not sending my son
to private school. huh?

679
00:37:07.615 --> 00:37:12.482
No. But you'd be a lousy father
if you didn't torture yourself about it.

680
00:37:15.053 --> 00:37:17.543
Thanks.

681
00:37:17.655 --> 00:37:19.781
Oh. hold on. Booth.

682
00:37:19.889 --> 00:37:23.380
I reviewed the placement
of the shotgun pellet wounds.

683
00:37:23.492 --> 00:37:26.426
The trajectory indicates
that whoever shot Cal...

684
00:37:26.527 --> 00:37:28.926
was significantly shorter than Mrs. King.

685
00:37:29.029 --> 00:37:30.962
I don't think she did it.

686
00:37:31.063 --> 00:37:33.656
<i>Right. Thanks.</i>

687
00:37:44.838 --> 00:37:49.035
<i>You see. we got a court order
for your cell phone and your laptop.</i>

688
00:37:49.141 --> 00:37:52.631
You know. nothing you text or I.M.
is really private.

689
00:37:52.742 --> 00:37:54.868
<i>- I knew that.
- Really?</i>

690
00:37:54.977 --> 00:37:56.967
Because. you know.
we checked your text messages...

691
00:37:57.078 --> 00:38:00.638
<i>and we found one from a couple weeks ago
that's a little weird.</i>

692
00:38:00.747 --> 00:38:03.681
<i>This one is from
the night that Cal was killed.</i>

693
00:38:03.783 --> 00:38:06.216
<i>"Bring it over now. Parents both gone. '"</i>

694
00:38:06.318 --> 00:38:08.842
- Stop this. Richard.
- No. I want to hear it.

695
00:38:08.953 --> 00:38:12.182
<i>It turns out that Alexa here
had a project due on Ancient Greece.</i>

696
00:38:12.287 --> 00:38:15.016
Mmm. You spent three days
complaining about it.

697
00:38:15.123 --> 00:38:18.285
- Why didn't you just do it?
- I didn't want to. It was stupid.

698
00:38:18.391 --> 00:38:20.324
Stupid? Oh.

699
00:38:20.426 --> 00:38:22.552
How about this one?
"'De' manny will do it.

700
00:38:22.660 --> 00:38:25.059
He can't say no. He works for me. "

701
00:38:25.162 --> 00:38:26.992
<i>But Cal really did say no. didn't he?</i>

702
00:38:27.096 --> 00:38:30.223
<i>Because you paid a hundred bucks to
somebody else to do it for you.</i>

703
00:38:30.332 --> 00:38:33.892
Someone by the name of Dakota Shearcroft?

704
00:38:34.001 --> 00:38:37.230
<i>- Oh. my God.
- Yeah. I think it's a stupid name too.</i>

705
00:38:37.336 --> 00:38:39.530
<i>So what happened? Did Cal catch you?</i>

706
00:38:39.637 --> 00:38:41.571
He threatened to tell the school.

707
00:38:41.672 --> 00:38:47.301
- Why wouldn't he have told us?
- Cal said you wouldn't do anything. You never do.

708
00:38:47.408 --> 00:38:50.171
- He said I had to learn a lesson.
- What kind of lesson?

709
00:38:50.277 --> 00:38:53.211
They would've kicked me out of school.
Zero tolerance.

710
00:38:55.514 --> 00:38:57.845
My friends are at that school.

711
00:38:59.316 --> 00:39:02.340
I got the gun out of the garage.
and I shot him.

712
00:39:05.086 --> 00:39:07.417
Mom came home and saw.

713
00:39:12.090 --> 00:39:14.717
I drove the body to the country house...

714
00:39:14.825 --> 00:39:17.452
<i>took it into the woods and burned it.</i>

715
00:39:23.230 --> 00:39:26.528
She's my daughter. I had to protect her.

716
00:39:26.633 --> 00:39:30.226
<i>No. You did what you always do-
make everything bad go away.</i>

717
00:39:30.334 --> 00:39:34.429
<i>It's counterproductive to raise children
in a world without consequences.</i>

718
00:39:34.538 --> 00:39:37.266
Well. it looks like little Alexa here
is gonna have to come face to face...

719
00:39:37.372 --> 00:39:40.340
<i>with some pretty nasty consequences.</i>

720
00:39:40.440 --> 00:39:44.535
But not the kind of consequences
you're gonna have to face. Mrs. King.

721
00:39:55.850 --> 00:39:58.943
Okay. Parker. this is called
the candy mint soda experiment.

722
00:39:59.052 --> 00:40:01.178
<i>You can do this for your dad at home.</i>

723
00:40:01.287 --> 00:40:04.084
So Mom copped to conspiracy
to avoid trial.

724
00:40:04.189 --> 00:40:06.088
She'll spend some time in prison.

725
00:40:06.190 --> 00:40:07.918
- How much?
- Not enough.

726
00:40:08.024 --> 00:40:09.957
In my opinion. she ruined
that little girl's life.

727
00:40:10.058 --> 00:40:12.457
What about the little girl?

728
00:40:12.560 --> 00:40:15.960
Removed from the family.
Institutionalized for a couple years.

729
00:40:16.062 --> 00:40:20.520
Then it's back to ponies and tennis lessons?

730
00:40:20.631 --> 00:40:23.963
- Mm-hmm.
- Well. Caroline should charge her as an adult.

731
00:40:24.067 --> 00:40:26.261
She's 11 years old. Bones.

732
00:40:26.368 --> 00:40:28.198
Yeah. well. she's old enough
to speak Chinese.

733
00:40:28.303 --> 00:40:30.202
Uh. they don't take that into account.

734
00:40:30.304 --> 00:40:33.431
I tell ya. man. when
that little girl killed Cal Warren...

735
00:40:33.539 --> 00:40:36.200
she killed the best father she had.

736
00:40:36.308 --> 00:40:39.037
- Well. sometimes it's hard to appreciate what you've got.
- Mmm.

737
00:40:39.143 --> 00:40:41.042
- You put the mints in the tube.
- Okay.

738
00:40:41.144 --> 00:40:43.976
<i>Look at him. Parker's
never liked science before.</i>

739
00:40:44.079 --> 00:40:46.410
<i>So are you still thinking
of sending him to Woodbury?</i>

740
00:40:46.514 --> 00:40:48.538
I torched the application.

741
00:40:48.648 --> 00:40:51.548
I'm thinking there's something
to be said for middle class.

742
00:40:51.650 --> 00:40:53.549
<i>That's right. Put them all in there.</i>

743
00:40:53.651 --> 00:40:56.881
- You sure he's gonna be all right?
- Sure.

744
00:40:56.987 --> 00:40:59.113
<i>- You're doing a great job. Parker.
- Well. probably.</i>

745
00:40:59.222 --> 00:41:02.247
Probably?
Like. what do you mean "probably"?

746
00:41:02.357 --> 00:41:04.381
I mean. what the hell they doing anyways?

747
00:41:04.491 --> 00:41:07.925
Disrupting the surface tension
of a two-liter cola.

748
00:41:08.026 --> 00:41:09.993
Right.

749
00:41:10.094 --> 00:41:12.960
Is that the last one?
All right. Put it in the tube.

750
00:41:13.063 --> 00:41:15.428
And then we'll both take a step back.

751
00:41:15.531 --> 00:41:17.589
Don't fire Max. Let him keep his job.

752
00:41:17.699 --> 00:41:19.633
You know. he's a teacher.
He's not a janitor.

753
00:41:19.734 --> 00:41:24.260
I can't overlook the sanctity
of the forensic lab. Booth.

754
00:41:24.370 --> 00:41:27.429
Yeah. Maybe you can overlook it for me?

755
00:41:27.538 --> 00:41:30.995
- For you?
- Yeah.

756
00:41:31.107 --> 00:41:34.564
- Personal favor.
- What. like a partner thing?

757
00:41:36.010 --> 00:41:38.170
Partner thing.

758
00:41:41.513 --> 00:41:43.412
I know you. Booth.

759
00:41:43.514 --> 00:41:46.448
You're trying to do me a favor
by telling me it's a favor for you.

760
00:41:46.550 --> 00:41:49.109
No. Mm-mmm. No. I-

761
00:41:49.218 --> 00:41:51.583
I can't afford that school.

762
00:41:51.686 --> 00:41:53.653
I can't enrich Parker.

763
00:41:53.754 --> 00:41:56.517
Not with the science thing. but you can.

764
00:41:56.623 --> 00:41:58.749
Max can.

765
00:42:02.592 --> 00:42:05.321
<i>Whoa!</i>

766
00:42:05.427 --> 00:42:08.327
That was so cool! Can we do it again?

767
00:42:08.429 --> 00:42:10.862
<i>Daddy. Daddy! Look what I did!
I blew it up!</i>

768
00:42:10.964 --> 00:42:14.160
Yeah. I saw. little man.

769
00:42:16.635 --> 00:42:18.534
<i>That's not magic. That's-</i>

770
00:42:18.636 --> 00:42:21.331
<i>- Max K.. the science way!
- Look at my dad.</i>

771
00:42:23.705 --> 00:42:26.036
Look at my little boy there with your dad.

772
00:42:31.977 --> 00:42:34.274
Okay.

773
00:42:34.379 --> 00:42:36.278
Yes.

774
00:42:36.380 --> 00:42:38.279
- All right.
- Thanks. Bones.

775
00:42:40.082 --> 00:42:43.481
<i># Change the way I feel #</i>

776
00:42:43.584 --> 00:42:46.177
Thanks.

777
00:42:46.286 --> 00:42:48.253
<i># And the sun will rise #</i>

778
00:42:48.353 --> 00:42:51.879
<i># The sun will save
from the night #</i>

779
00:42:51.989 --> 00:42:54.513
<i># The sun will change #</i>

780
00:42:54.624 --> 00:42:57.558
<i># Change the way I feel ##</i>

781
00:43:34.515 --> 00:43:35.879
What's that mean?

